# SSH boundary for intake submit (plan p2-wake-on-work, B11).
#
# EXAMPLE — parameterized notes for the EXISTING bridge SSH public-key
# boundary. This task edits no authorized_keys file and enrolls no trust; the
# operator applies changes through existing owner routes only.
#
# Goal: a submit client authenticates at the bridge with its SSH key; the key
# can ONLY run the submit shim (forced command), and the shim learns the
# PRINCIPAL from what sshd actually presented — never from client input.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. sshd_config (bridge) — expose the presented-key identity to the session
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   ExposeAuthInfo yes
#
# With this, every session gets SSH_USER_AUTH=<path> in its environment (the
# variable name OpenSSH actually exports — official 10.0p2 session.c:1168);
# the per-session file (mkstemp'd, session-user-owned, 0600, unlinked at
# session end) contains one line per successful method. For key
# authentication that line is the METHOD followed by the KEY TEXT —
# `publickey <algorithm> <base64-key-blob>` (official 10.0p2 auth2.c
# auth2_update_session_info + sshkey.c sshkey_format_text) — for example:
#
#   publickey ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA…
#
# sshd never writes a fingerprint into this file. submit.mjs derives the
# SHA256 fingerprint the stock utility reports (`ssh-keygen -lf`: SHA256 over
# the raw wire-blob bytes, base64 with padding trimmed) from those bytes and
# resolves THAT against the operator-owned principals.json. A bare
# `publickey SHA256:…` line is NOT the sshd format; the shim still accepts it
# from the same trusted file only as a legacy compatibility form (pre-R10
# fixtures/tooling), documented as non-native.
#
# The forced-command wrapper assigns that real per-session path to the
# EXISTING submit config seam AIF_INTAKE_RELAY_SSH_AUTH_INFO_PATH (after any
# operator config file is sourced, so a config value can never override or
# blank it); submit.mjs reads the identity from that seam FIRST (what sshd
# ACTUALLY presented) and falls back to the SSH_USER_AUTH path only when no
# explicit path is configured. There is no static per-session filename to
# hardcode. Reload sshd after changing sshd_config (existing owner
# procedure).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. authorized_keys entry for the submit principal (bridge relay user)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The forced command MUST invoke node explicitly — the .mjs shim carries no
# shebang, so a bare path would fail to exec (ENOEXEC) and every submission
# would fail closed with no shim output at all:
#
#   restrict,command="node <install-dir>/scripts/workstation/intake-relay/submit.mjs" ssh-ed25519 <COMMENT>
#
# (An equivalent wrapper script with a shebang that execs node on the same
# path is also valid.) `restrict` disables pty/agent/X11/port-forwarding;
# `command=` forces the shim regardless of the client's requested command.
# The client submits by piping the JSON payload on stdin:
#
#   ssh -T <relay-user>@<bridge> < payload.json
#
# The shim ignores SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND and any client-claimed principal
# fields; it relays the truthful receipt/refusal JSON on stdout.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Documented FALLBACK: per-key environment variable (weaker trust)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# When ExposeAuthInfo cannot be enabled, a per-key environment variable can
# carry the principalId. Requires sshd_config — as the scoped PATTERN LIST,
# never the global yes (which would let every accepted client request
# environment variables):
#
#   PermitUserEnvironment AIF_RELAY_PRINCIPAL
#
# authorized_keys entry:
#
#   restrict,command="…/submit.mjs",environment="AIF_RELAY_PRINCIPAL=<principal-id>" ssh-ed25519 <COMMENT>
#
# and config:
#
#   AIF_INTAKE_RELAY_PRINCIPAL_ENV_VAR=AIF_RELAY_PRINCIPAL
#
# TRUST CAVEAT (R1): the variable is operator-set per key, but it is a claim
# configured alongside the key — not a proof of the key sshd presented. The
# ExposeAuthInfo path is preferred; an arbitrary inherited environment
# variable is never sufficient by itself, and the shim refuses with
# `principal_unknown` when neither channel resolves a known principal.
